{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are generally too high versus realized prices (often by ~1–4 EUR), especially during downtrends; apply a downward level shift to the whole path.",
    "Downtrend underestimation (slope too shallow): when recent history is falling, the model does not drop fast enough; increase negative drift in the first 10–15 days (stronger early-path decline).",
    "Horizon-shaped error: largest overprediction tends to occur in the mid-horizon (≈days 8–20), with smaller errors at the very front and sometimes partial catch-up late; apply a hump-shaped downward correction centered mid-horizon.",
    "Over-optimistic rebound/mean reversion: after sharp declines, the model expects a quicker bounce than realized; dampen mean-reversion strength (reduce rebound amplitude) unless history shows a clear reversal.",
    "Turning-point lag: the model can be late to recognize regime shifts (keeps forecasting higher levels even as realized breaks lower); add a momentum/regime filter—if last 5–10 days momentum is negative, cap any forecasted upticks and enforce monotone/non-increasing segments early.",
    "Volatility compression with upside skew: forecast path is smoother and biased upward; widen downside risk (lower quantile adjustment) more than upside, particularly in bearish regimes."
  ]
}
